WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.883
Two monkeyflowers can be crossed by hand to give hybrid seed.

2
00:00:07.803 --> 00:00:10.363
One is pink, visited mostly by bumblebees.

3
00:00:11.263 --> 00:00:14.023
The other is red, visited mostly by hummingbirds.

4
00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:17.783
Yet in the wild, almost no genes pass between them.

5
00:00:18.423 --> 00:00:20.063
That is what makes them two species.

6
00:00:21.163 --> 00:00:25.703
Speciation is the evolution of barriers that cut gene flow between populations.

7
00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:31.103
And for these flowers, the barriers grew mostly out of adapting to different places

8
00:00:31.103 --> 00:00:32.403
and different pollinators.

9
00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:37.307
A reproductive barrier is anything that reduces gene flow.

10
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:39.887
Barriers are sorted by when they act.

11
00:00:40.647 --> 00:00:45.047
Where the plants grow, and which pollinators visit them, act before the zygote, the

12
00:00:45.047 --> 00:00:48.647
fertilized egg. Low hybrid fertility acts after.

13
00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:54.027
No single barrier here is complete, and most of the isolation comes before any

14
00:00:54.027 --> 00:00:55.027
hybrid forms.

15
00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:57.950
These barriers were not built on purpose.

16
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:03.050
One species is adapted to lower elevations, the other to higher ones.

17
00:01:03.890 --> 00:01:06.950
One flower suits bumblebees, the other suits hummingbirds.

18
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:12.010
So the plants rarely meet, and a pollinator carries little pollen between them.

19
00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:15.290
Isolation is a side effect of adaptation.

20
00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.950
This is ecological speciation, driven by divergent selection.

21
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:23.443
How do we know selection did this, and not chance?

22
00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:28.983
Lakes in British Columbia each hold a bottom-feeding and an open-water stickleback,

23
00:01:29.423 --> 00:01:32.183
and each lake's pair seems to have arisen on its own.

24
00:01:33.043 --> 00:01:37.803
In mating trials, fish of different forms rarely mated, but same-form fish from

25
00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:39.083
different lakes mated readily.

26
00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:42.623
Isolation followed ecology, lake after lake.

27
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:45.123
Barriers act in sequence.

28
00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:50.143
Each one can only block the gene flow that earlier barriers left behind.

29
00:01:51.063 --> 00:01:56.023
So, if they act independently, the fractions that get through multiply, and total

30
00:01:56.023 --> 00:01:58.203
isolation is one minus their product.

31
00:01:59.123 --> 00:02:00.603
Never just add the raw strengths.

32
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:05.483
Their sum can be more than one, which means nothing as a fraction of gene flow.

33
00:02:06.467 --> 00:02:09.787
Where speciation happens is a separate question from how.

34
00:02:10.607 --> 00:02:15.507
A sea or a mountain range can split a population, which is allopatric speciation.

35
00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:20.347
Or forms can diverge in one place, which is sympatric speciation.

36
00:02:21.347 --> 00:02:24.347
Living together today does not prove a sympatric start.

37
00:02:25.247 --> 00:02:29.767
For crater-lake cichlids in Nicaragua, genome-wide models supported divergence

38
00:02:29.767 --> 00:02:33.107
inside the lake, with some genes arriving from outside.

39
00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:34.107
www .cichlids .com

40
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.910
There is also a faster route.

41
00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.870
In plants especially, a hybrid can double its whole genome.

42
00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:45.410
The new polyploid lineage is partly isolated from its parents in a single

43
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:48.850
generation. Either way, the result is the same.

44
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.950
Barriers cut gene flow, and a new lineage evolves on its own.
